  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nThere has been little change to the thinking regarding the\nlarge-scale pattern. An approaching mid-level ridge is progged\nto flatten as it crosses the Great Lakes tonight and tomorrow\nmorning. Meanwhile an Upper Plains shortwave trough will meander\neastward with time, eventually digging into the Middle Ohio\nValley by midweek. This keeps a favorable storm track across our\nregion into at least midweek, with periods of quasi-zonal flow\nsupporting mid-level disturbances tracking across the Upper Ohio\nValley.\n\nAfter a few morning rounds of heavy rain in eastern Ohio and\nsouthwest Pennsylvania, lighter rain from a decaying MCS will\ntrack across the region through the early afternoon. This should\nnot exacerbate any local flooding concerns initially. With a\nstationary boundary draped across the region which will nudge\nsouthward towards the Mason-Dixon line this evening, deeper\ninstability (also inhibited by abundant cloud cover) should\nremain mainly south of the region, largely mitigating any severe\nrisks.\n\nThat said, deep and anomalous moisture (PBZ 12Z PWAT of 1.92 \ninches represents a September 20th climatologic record) will be\nmaintained into Monday, especially from Pittsburgh on south.\nWhile a relative lull in activity is pictured during the very\nlate afternoon/evening hours, the next vort max/ripple\n(currently near Chicago) is expected to approach our area after\nmidnight and cross through Monday morning. HREF means suggest\n0.5 to 1.5 inches is possible near and south of Pittsburgh from\nthis round (24 hour total through 06Z Tuesday), but maximum\nfields suggest several stripes of 2 to 3 inches is possible -\ncertainly reasonable given the high PWAT and nearly saturated\nsoundings. Given the very saturated ground already in place, a\nFlood Watch for flash flooding has already been issued for this\narea through noon Monday. The exact placement of the heaviest\ntotals remains a bit uncertain, and will depend on the progress\nof the front as well as other mesoscale details. \n\nCAMS then suggest another general lull Monday afternoon into the\nevening, and this is why the Flood Watch only extends through\nnoon Monday for now. The pattern thereafter suggests additional\nrounds of rain, as noted above. Decreasing precipitable water\nvalues with time and very muted instability levels suggest a\nlesser threat of flash flooding with time. However, long-\nduration, lighter rain may still support more of a overflow\nflooding/river flooding risk into the midweek period. NBM \n48-hour rain totals through 8 PM Wednesday still suggest the \npotential for 1-2 inches of rain southeast of a DUJ/PIT/PKB\nline. Again, the location of highest totals may depend on the\nfate of the slow-moving frontal boundary that will be hovering\nnear the WV/PA border. In any case, flood threats will be\nmaintained through at least Wednesday. Some showers may even\nlinger into Thursday as the main approaching trough possibly\ncloses off into an upper low. There is still hope for a drier\npattern for Friday and next weekend.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nCool temperatures remain the second part of the story this week.\nGiven the abundant cloud cover and stubborn east/northeast flow,\nhigher ridge locations may have trouble reaching 60 degrees\nTuesday through Thursday, while lower and mid 60s will be common\nwest of the higher elevations. While minimum temperatures will\nremain fairly close to normal, daytime highs may run some 10\ndegrees below climatology during the midweek period.",
